Pros: Large input impedance allows measuring very small currents, and gain is easily changed with a single external resistor (R_g).
Cons: Common-mode voltage is limited to the supply voltage (V_CM <= V_s).

{{image_1}}

Pros: Can be used for high or low-side sensing, and can tolerate very large common-mode voltages due to its input resistor divider.
Cons: The resistor network loads the system, requiring the system impedance to be significantly smaller than the DA's input impedance.

{{image_1}}

Pros: A unique floating input stage allows V_CM to be much greater than V_s with large input impedance. A precision integrated gain network maximizes accuracy and minimizes drift.
Cons: Usually only offered at fixed gains, and greater accuracy is achieved at higher currents.
